package com.morphlabs.morph.core.academy.deck

import com.morphlabs.morph.core.academy.Card
import com.morphlabs.morph.core.academy.Track

internal object DealsCards {
    private val T = Track.DEALS
    val cards: List<Card> = listOf(
        // ---- Level 1 · Rookie (adds to the 7 starter cards) -----------------------------------------
        concept("sh-equity", T, 1, "What does owning equity in a business actually mean?",
            "You own a slice of the company: its future profits, its sale value, and usually a vote. 10% of a company worth Ksh 5M is Ksh 500,000 on paper, but it only becomes cash through dividends or when someone buys your shares."),
        concept("sh-debt-vs-equity", T, 1, "What's the main difference between a loan and selling equity?",
            "A loan is repaid; equity is shared ownership for good. A loan ends when you repay it, with interest. Selling equity has no repayments, but the investor shares in every shilling of future value."),
        quiz("sh-post-money-basic", T, 1, "An investor puts Ksh 1M into a company worth Ksh 4M before the money. What's it worth just after?",
            listOf("Ksh 5M", "Ksh 4M", "Ksh 3M"), 0,
            "Pre-money (4M) + new cash (1M) = post-money 5M. The investor owns 1M ÷ 5M = 20%."),
        concept("sh-get-paid", T, 1, "You own 10% of a profitable private company. How do you get cash from it?",
            "Dividends, or selling your shares to a buyer. Shareholders aren't paid automatically. Dividends must be declared, and private shares are hard to sell. Agree how you'll get paid before investing."),
        concept("sh-investor-wants", T, 1, "What does an equity investor want in return for their money?",
            "A big multiple when the company grows or is sold, not monthly payments. That's why they ask \"how do I exit?\" A steady business paying its owner well can be a great business but a poor deal for outside shareholders."),

        // ---- Level 2 · Hustler ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        quiz("sh-ask-implied", T, 2, "A founder asks \"Ksh 500,000 for 25%\". What post-money valuation does that imply?",
            listOf("Ksh 2M", "Ksh 1.25M", "Ksh 500,000"), 0,
            "500,000 ÷ 0.25 = Ksh 2M after the money goes in. Always turn an ask into a valuation, then ask if the business is worth that."),
        quiz("sh-pre-from-ask", T, 2, "A founder asks Ksh 2M for 20%. What's the pre-money valuation?",
            listOf("Ksh 8M", "Ksh 10M", "Ksh 12M"), 0,
            "Post-money = 2M ÷ 0.20 = 10M. Pre-money = 10M − the 2M coming in = 8M. Investors and founders often argue past each other by mixing these."),
        quiz("sh-percent-for", T, 2, "Your company is worth Ksh 3M pre-money and you raise Ksh 1M. What share do you sell?",
            listOf("25%", "33%", "20%"), 0,
            "Post-money = 4M, so the investor's 1M buys 1 ÷ 4 = 25%. (33% would be using the pre-money by mistake.)"),
        quiz("sh-compare-offers", T, 2, "Offer A: Ksh 2M for 40%. Offer B: Ksh 1.5M for 20%. Which values your company higher?",
            listOf("Offer B (Ksh 7.5M vs Ksh 5M)", "Offer A: it brings more cash", "They're the same"), 0,
            "A: 2M ÷ 0.40 = 5M. B: 1.5M ÷ 0.20 = 7.5M. More cash isn't a better deal if it costs twice the ownership. Also weigh what each investor brings."),
        quiz("sh-loan-vs-equity-cost", T, 2, "Ksh 1M loan at 15% a year, or 20% equity in a business making Ksh 2M profit a year. Year-one cost?",
            listOf("Loan ≈ Ksh 150,000; equity ≈ Ksh 400,000 a year, forever", "The loan costs more",
                "Both cost the same"), 0,
            "Interest is 1M × 15% = 150,000. The investor's 20% claim is worth 400,000 of profit each year, with no end date. Equity is the priciest money if you succeed."),
        quiz("sh-royalty-calc", T, 2, "Royalty deal: Ksh 1M for 5% of sales until Ksh 1.5M is paid back. Sales are Ksh 500,000 a month. How long?",
            listOf("60 months", "24 months", "12 months"), 0,
            "5% of 500,000 = 25,000 a month; 1.5M ÷ 25,000 = 60 months. Faster sales make the investor's return come sooner, so their effective rate rises with your success."),
        concept("sh-when-equity", T, 2, "When does equity make more sense than debt?",
            "When cash flow is uncertain or years away (no revenue yet), when repayments would choke the business, or when the investor brings real skills or contacts. Debt suits steady cash flow; equity suits risky, fast-growth bets."),
        quiz("sh-owner-labour", T, 2, "A kibanda makes Ksh 300,000 profit a year, but the owner works 12 hours a day. A manager costs Ksh 240,000 a year. True profit?",
            listOf("Ksh 60,000", "Ksh 300,000", "Ksh 540,000"), 0,
            "An investor buys what the business earns without the owner's free labour: 300,000 − 240,000 = 60,000. Value that, not the headline number."),
        concept("sh-sweat-equity", T, 2, "A developer offers to build your app for 30% of the company instead of cash. Main risk?",
            "You give a big permanent stake for a one-off job. Shares outlive the work. If they leave after launch, they still own 30%. Use vesting, pay part in cash, or offer a smaller stake."),
        quiz("sh-majority", T, 2, "In a Kenyan company, what does holding 51% of the votes usually give you?",
            listOf("Power to pass ordinary resolutions", "Power to change the articles alone",
                "No extra power at all"), 0,
            "Ordinary resolutions need a simple majority (over 50%). Special resolutions, like changing the articles, need 75%. Watch both lines when you sell shares.",
            source = "Companies Act 2015"),
        concept("sh-shareholders-agreement", T, 2, "What is a shareholders' agreement for?",
            "A private contract among owners covering what the articles don't: who decides what, what happens if someone leaves or dies, who may buy shares first, and how disputes are settled. Write it while everyone's still friends."),

        // ---- Level 3 · Pro -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        quiz("sh-dilution-multi", T, 3, "You own 60%. The company issues new shares equal to 20% of the total after the round. You now own…",
            listOf("48%", "40%", "50%"), 0,
            "Everyone keeps 80% of their old stake: 60% × 0.8 = 48%. Dilution multiplies; it doesn't subtract."),
        quiz("sh-dilution-value", T, 3, "You own 50% of a company. It raises Ksh 5M at a Ksh 15M pre-money valuation. Your stake after, and its value?",
            listOf("37.5%, worth Ksh 7.5M", "50%, worth Ksh 10M", "40%, worth Ksh 8M"), 0,
            "Post-money is 20M; the investor gets 25%, so you keep 50% × 0.75 = 37.5% of 20M = 7.5M. Before, your half of 15M was 7.5M too: a smaller slice of a bigger cake."),
        quiz("sh-option-pool", T, 3, "A Ksh 8M pre-money offer requires a 10% employee option pool created \"pre-money\". Who pays for it?",
            listOf("The existing founders, via extra dilution", "The new investor", "The employees"), 0,
            "The pool comes out of the pre-money, so only current owners are diluted. Your effective pre-money is lower than 8M. This is the \"option pool shuffle\"."),
        quiz("sh-revenue-multiple", T, 3, "A software firm with Ksh 10M yearly revenue sells for 3× revenue. Price?",
            listOf("Ksh 30M", "Ksh 3.3M", "Ksh 13M"), 0,
            "10M × 3 = 30M. Revenue multiples suit fast-growing, high-margin firms. A low-margin trading business would sell for far less per shilling of sales."),
        quiz("sh-ebitda", T, 3, "EBITDA means earnings before…",
            listOf("Interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation", "Expenses, borrowing, inventory and debt",
                "Equity, bonuses, interest and dividends"), 0,
            "It's a rough measure of operating profit before financing and accounting choices. Buyers often price a business as a multiple of it, then subtract debt."),
        quiz("sh-ev-equity", T, 3, "A firm is worth 5× its Ksh 4M EBITDA. It has Ksh 6M debt and Ksh 1M cash. What are the shares worth?",
            listOf("Ksh 15M", "Ksh 20M", "Ksh 27M"), 0,
            "Enterprise value = 5 × 4M = 20M. Equity value = 20M − 6M debt + 1M cash = 15M. Debt belongs to lenders first."),
        quiz("sh-pe", T, 3, "An NSE share earns Ksh 2 per share a year and trades at Ksh 20. Its P/E ratio is…",
            listOf("10×", "40×", "0.1×"), 0,
            "Price ÷ earnings per share = 20 ÷ 2 = 10. You're paying 10 years of today's profit. A higher P/E means buyers expect growth."),
        concept("sh-why-multiples", T, 3, "Why might one business sell for 3× profit and another for 30×?",
            "Growth, risk and quality. Higher multiples go to fast growth, recurring revenue, high margins, many customers and no reliance on the owner. A one-shop business tied to its founder gets a low multiple."),
        quiz("sh-vesting", T, 3, "The most common founder vesting schedule in startups is…",
            listOf("4 years with a 1-year cliff", "1 year, no cliff", "10 years, monthly"), 0,
            "Shares are earned over 4 years. Leave before 12 months and you get none; at the cliff you get 25%, then the rest monthly. It protects co-founders from someone who quits early."),
        quiz("sh-vesting-calc", T, 3, "A co-founder with 30% on 4-year vesting (1-year cliff) leaves after 18 months. Vested stake?",
            listOf("11.25%", "7.5%", "0%", "30%"), 0,
            "18 of 48 months are vested: 30% × 18 ÷ 48 = 11.25%. The other 18.75% goes back to the company for a replacement."),
        concept("sh-cap-table", T, 3, "What is a cap table, and why keep it clean?",
            "The list of who owns what: shares, options, SAFEs and notes, shown \"fully diluted\". Investors run from messy tables, like an ex-co-founder holding 40% or dozens of tiny holders with unclear rights."),
        quiz("sh-fully-diluted", T, 3, "Founders hold 8M shares, investors 1.5M, and an unissued option pool 0.5M. Investors' fully diluted share?",
            listOf("15%", "15.8%", "18.75%"), 0,
            "Fully diluted counts the pool too: 1.5M ÷ 10M = 15%. Ignoring the pool gives 15.8%, flattering the numbers."),
        quiz("sh-carta-dilution", T, 3, "Carta's data: a median US seed or Series A round sells about how much of the company?",
            listOf("About 20%", "About 5%", "About 50%"), 0,
            "Medians were about 20% in 2024 and closer to 18% by mid-2026. An investor asking for 40%+ at seed is far outside the norm.",
            source = "Carta, 2024–2026", timeSensitive = true),

        // ---- Level 4 · Shark ------------------------------------------------------------------------
        concept("sh-safe", T, 4, "What is a SAFE?",
            "A Simple Agreement for Future Equity (Y Combinator, 2013): money now, shares later at the next priced round, usually with a valuation cap and/or discount. No interest, no due date. YC's 2018 post-money SAFE makes ownership easier to calculate.",
            source = "Y Combinator"),
        quiz("sh-safe-cap", T, 4, "A Ksh 2M post-money SAFE has a Ksh 20M cap. The next round prices the company far higher. The SAFE converts to about…",
            listOf("10%", "Less than 10%, since the price rose", "20%"), 0,
            "With a post-money cap, ownership is cash ÷ cap = 2M ÷ 20M = 10% (before the new round's dilution). The cap protects early believers when the price jumps."),
        quiz("sh-note-discount", T, 4, "A convertible note has a 20% discount. Series A investors pay Ksh 100 a share. The note converts at…",
            listOf("Ksh 80 a share", "Ksh 120 a share", "Ksh 100 a share"), 0,
            "100 × (1 − 0.20) = 80. The discount rewards taking risk earlier. Notes also accrue interest, which converts into extra shares."),
        quiz("sh-cap-vs-discount", T, 4, "A SAFE has a Ksh 10M cap and 20% discount. The next round is priced at Ksh 30M. Which sets the SAFE's price?",
            listOf("The cap: it gives the lower price", "The discount", "Both, multiplied together"), 0,
            "The discount gives a 24M price; the cap gives 10M. The investor gets whichever is better for them, here the cap: they buy at a third of the new price."),
        concept("sh-note-vs-safe", T, 4, "How does a convertible note differ from a SAFE?",
            "A note is debt, with interest and a maturity date. If it matures before a priced round, the investor can in theory demand repayment, a real risk for a cash-poor startup. A SAFE isn't debt."),
        quiz("sh-liq-pref", T, 4, "Investor paid Ksh 5M for 25% with a 1× non-participating preference. Company sells for Ksh 12M. Investor gets…",
            listOf("Ksh 5M", "Ksh 3M", "Ksh 6.75M"), 0,
            "They choose the better of 1× their money (5M) or 25% of 12M (3M), so 5M. Founders share 7M. In weak exits, preferences decide who gets paid."),
        quiz("sh-participating", T, 4, "Same deal, but the preference is participating. On a Ksh 12M sale, the investor gets…",
            listOf("Ksh 6.75M", "Ksh 5M", "Ksh 3M"), 0,
            "Participating means money back plus a share of the rest: 5M + 25% × 7M = 6.75M. \"Double dipping\" is investor-friendly; push back on it."),
        quiz("sh-anti-dilution", T, 4, "\"Full-ratchet\" anti-dilution means that if a later round is priced lower…",
            listOf("The earlier investor's price drops to the new low price", "Founders get bonus shares",
                "The new round is cancelled"), 0,
            "Full ratchet can wipe out founders in a down round. Broad-based weighted average, which only partly adjusts, is the founder-friendlier norm."),
        concept("sh-pro-rata", T, 4, "What are pro-rata rights in a term sheet?",
            "The right (not the duty) to invest in future rounds to keep your percentage. An investor with 10% can buy 10% of the next round. Good for investors; too many holders can crowd out new ones."),
        concept("sh-board", T, 4, "Why can board seats matter as much as valuation?",
            "The board hires and fires the CEO, and approves budgets, new rounds and a sale. A common early board is 2 founders + 1 investor. Giving away board control early can cost you your company, even with majority shares."),
        concept("sh-drag-tag", T, 4, "What does a drag-along clause let shareholders do?",
            "It lets a majority force minorities to join a sale on the same terms. Drag-along stops one small holder blocking a sale. Its partner, tag-along, lets minorities join if the majority sells, so they aren't left behind."),
        quiz("sh-dd-redflag", T, 4, "Due diligence: which is the biggest red flag?",
            listOf("Pitch revenue doesn't match bank and M-Pesa statements", "Founder works from home",
                "There are three competitors", "Accounts are kept in Excel"), 0,
            "Numbers that don't reconcile point to sloppiness or dishonesty. Also check: unpaid KRA taxes, IP owned by a founder personally, unsigned contracts, related-party deals."),
        concept("sh-term-sheet-binding", T, 4, "Is a startup term sheet usually legally binding?",
            "Mostly not, except clauses like confidentiality and exclusivity. It's a summary of agreed deal terms. The binding documents come later (subscription and shareholders' agreements), but walking away from agreed terms damages your reputation."),
        concept("sh-batna", T, 4, "What is a BATNA, and why does it matter in a deal?",
            "Your Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement (Fisher & Ury, 1981). If your only option is this investor, you'll accept bad terms. Revenue, runway and other interested investors make your BATNA, and your bargaining power, stronger.",
            source = "Fisher & Ury, Getting to Yes, 1981"),

        // ---- Level 5 · Legend ----------------------------------------------------------------------
        quiz("sh-partech-2025", T, 5, "Partech: African tech raised about US\$4.1B (equity + debt) in 2025. Which country raised the most in total?",
            listOf("Kenya, about US\$1.04B", "Nigeria", "South Africa", "Egypt"), 0,
            "Kenya led, mostly through big debt rounds and 4 of the 9 megadeals. South Africa led on equity and deal count.",
            source = "Partech Africa, 2026", timeSensitive = true),
        quiz("sh-downturn", T, 5, "Partech's numbers: total African tech funding fell from about US\$6.5B in 2022 to what in 2024?",
            listOf("About US\$3.2B", "About US\$10B", "About US\$0.5B"), 0,
            "Roughly halved as global rates rose. Equity sat near US\$2.2B in 2023 and 2024, then rose to US\$2.4B in 2025 while debt hit a record US\$1.6B.",
            source = "Partech Africa, 2024–2026", timeSensitive = true),
        concept("sh-debt-rise", T, 5, "Why is debt a growing share of African startup funding?",
            "Asset-heavy models like pay-as-you-go solar, e-mobility and lending finance their assets with debt, which doesn't dilute owners. After 2022, equity investors got pickier, demanding a path to profit before writing cheques.",
            source = "Partech Africa, 2026", timeSensitive = true),
        quiz("sh-briter", T, 5, "Briter's 2025 report: what share of disclosed African funding went to the \"Big Four\" (SA, Kenya, Egypt, Nigeria)?",
            listOf("About 84%", "About 40%", "About 60%"), 0,
            "Kenya alone took about 29%. Fintech stayed the biggest sector and solar the most-funded category. Outside the Big Four, capital is scarce.",
            source = "Briter, 2026", timeSensitive = true),
        quiz("sh-ring", T, 5, "Ring (then DoorBot) left Shark Tank in 2013 with no deal. In 2018 Amazon bought it for roughly…",
            listOf("US\$1 billion (as reported)", "US\$10 million", "US\$100 million"), 0,
            "It asked US\$700,000 for 10% and turned down Kevin O'Leary's royalty-plus-equity offer. Even experts pass on winners; a \"no\" isn't a verdict.",
            source = "Fortune, 2025"),
        quiz("sh-scrub-daddy", T, 5, "Scrub Daddy asked Shark Tank for US\$100,000 for 10%. After a bidding war, Lori Greiner's deal was…",
            listOf("US\$200,000 for 20%", "US\$100,000 for 25%", "US\$1M for 50%"), 0,
            "Same US\$1M valuation, but twice the cash and a partner with TV-shopping distribution. The right investor can matter more than the price.",
            source = "Shark Tank, 2012"),
        quiz("sh-levi-roots", T, 5, "Dragons' Den, 2007: Levi Roots asked £50,000 for 20% of Reggae Reggae Sauce. He accepted £50,000 for…",
            listOf("40%", "20%", "10%"), 0,
            "Fumbled numbers halved his valuation, from £250,000 to £125,000. The Dragons' retail contacts still made it a hit. Know your numbers cold.",
            source = "BBC Dragons' Den, 2007"),
        quiz("sh-tangle-teezer", T, 5, "Which product did the Dragons reject in 2007, yet BIC bought in 2024?",
            listOf("Tangle Teezer", "Reggae Reggae Sauce", "Scrub Daddy", "Ring"), 0,
            "Shaun Pulfrey offered 15% for £80,000 and was mocked. The hairbrush went global, and BIC reportedly paid about £165M for it.",
            source = "Dragons' Den, 2007; press, 2024"),
        quiz("sh-power-law", T, 5, "Correlation Ventures studied 21,000+ US VC financings (2004–2013). How many returned less than the money invested?",
            listOf("About 65%", "About 10%", "About 35%"), 0,
            "Only about 4% returned 10× or more. Venture returns follow a power law: a few huge wins pay for many losses. Angels need many small bets.",
            source = "Correlation Ventures, via Seth Levine, 2014"),
        concept("sh-kaplan-stromberg", T, 5, "What did Kaplan & Strömberg (2003) find when they studied real VC contracts?",
            "VCs usually get more control when the company performs badly. Contracts split cash-flow, voting, board and liquidation rights separately, shifting control to investors when results disappoint and back to founders when they're good.",
            source = "Kaplan & Strömberg, 2003"),
        concept("sh-down-round", T, 5, "A startup raised at Ksh 100M post-money. Its next round is priced at Ksh 60M pre-money. What's that called?",
            "A down round. Value fell, so anti-dilution clauses kick in, earlier holders are diluted harder and morale suffers. Down rounds spread across Africa in 2023–2024."),
        concept("sh-invest-laundry", T, 5, "Would you invest? Laundry app: customers spend Ksh 2,000/month at 25% margin, stay 8 months, CAC Ksh 1,500. Asks Ksh 5M for 10%.",
            "LTV = 2,000 × 25% × 8 = 4,000, so LTV:CAC ≈ 2.7: borderline. The ask implies Ksh 50M post-money. If revenue is about Ksh 400,000 a month, that's over 10× yearly sales for a low-margin service. Counter lower, or wait for better retention."),
        concept("sh-invest-posho", T, 5, "Would you invest? A posho mill makes Ksh 1.2M net profit a year. The owner asks Ksh 1M for 25%.",
            "Implied value Ksh 4M ≈ 3.3× profit: reasonable for a small business. Your 25% = Ksh 300,000 a year, a 30% yield if profit holds. Check: is profit after paying the owner? Records? How will you be paid (dividend policy in writing)?"),
        you("sh-you-angel", T, 5, "Your net worth is {net_worth}. Why do experienced angels keep startup bets to a small slice of their wealth?",
            "Most startup investments lose money and a few win big. Angels spread small amounts across many deals and only invest what they could lose entirely, which is very different from {net_worth} in one deal."),
    )
}
